ttps:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/07\/20180524_152825.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The Soldiers National Cemetery is located just before the Evergreen Cemetery when coming from the Jennie Wade house.\" data-attachment-id=\"4020\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The Soldiers National Cemetery is located just before the Evergreen Cemetery when coming from the Jennie Wade house.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/07\/20180524_152825\/3726352132.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The Soldiers National Cemetery is located just before the Evergreen Cemetery when coming from the Jennie Wade house.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/07\/20180524_164544-1.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The Virginia Memorial was the first confederate state memorial to be erected in Gettysburg. Dedicated in 1917, the memorial depicts General Robert E. Lee atop his horse Traveller and the various men that participated in the war. Among the professions shown at the base of the monument is a business man, an artist and a farmer.\" data-attachment-id=\"4021\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The Virginia Memorial was the first confederate state memorial to be erected in Gettysburg. Dedicated in 1917, the memorial depicts General Robert E. Lee atop his horse Traveller and the various men that participated in the war. Among the professions shown at the base of the monument is a business man, an artist and a farmer.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/07\/20180524_164544-1\/3317966337.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The Virginia Memorial was the first confederate state memorial to be erected in Gettysburg. Dedicated in 1917, the memorial depicts General Robert E. Lee atop his horse Traveller and the various men that participated in the war. Among the professions shown at the base of the monument is a business man, an artist and a farmer.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/07\/20180524_170217.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Various memorials and brigade markers lay across the Gettysburg fields, honoring those who have fought and fallen in the war.\" data-attachment-id=\"4022\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Various memorials and brigade markers lay across the Gettysburg fields, honoring those who have fought and fallen in the war.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/07\/20180524_170217\/3002168209.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Various memorials and brigade markers lay across the Gettysburg fields, honoring those who have fought and fallen in the war.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/07\/20180524_175006.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"What look like mere fields today were at one point blood-soaked battlegrounds of the Battle of Gettysburg.\" data-attachment-id=\"4024\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"What look like mere fields today were at one point blood-soaked battlegrounds of the Battle of Gettysburg.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/07\/20180524_175006\/464174268.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">What look like mere fields today were at one point blood-soaked battlegrounds of the Battle of Gettysburg.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/07\/20180524_173140.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Brigadier General Samuel Wiley Crawford was a commander of the Pennsylvania Reserves. He had a strong emotional attachment to the land on which the Battle of Gettysburg was fought. Instead of letting it be developed, Crawford bought the land in order to preserve it. Later, he became the director of the Gettysburg Battlefield Memorial Association (GBMA).\n&lt;p&gt;This stands at the foot of Little Round Top and at the entrance to the Devil&#039;s Den. Little Round Top was controlled by the Union forces and was crucial to maintaining the upper hand. Devil&#039;s Den was so named because it was one of the deadliest locations to be in, given that snipers could easily pick the enemy off from atop Little Round Top.&lt;\/p&gt;\" data-attachment-id=\"4023\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Brigadier General Samuel Wiley Crawford was a commander of the Pennsylvania Reserves. He had a strong emotional attachment to the land on which the Battle of Gettysburg was fought. Instead of letting it be developed, Crawford bought the land in order to preserve it. Later, he became the director of the Gettysburg Battlefield Memorial Association (GBMA).\n&lt;p&gt;This stands at the foot of Little Round Top and at the entrance to the Devil&#039;s Den. Little Round Top was controlled by the Union forces and was crucial to maintaining the upper hand. Devil&#039;s Den was so named because it was one of the deadliest locations to be in, given that snipers could easily pick the enemy off from atop Little Round Top.&lt;\/p&gt;\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/07\/20180524_173140\/3664597656.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Brigadier General Samuel Wiley Crawford was a commander of the Pennsylvania Reserves. He had a strong emotional attachment to the land on which the Battle of Gettysburg was fought. Instead of letting it be developed, Crawford bought the land in order to preserve it. Later, he became the director of the Gettysburg Battlefield Memorial Association (GBMA).\n<p>This stands at the foot of Little Round Top and at the entrance to the Devil's Den. Little Round Top was controlled by the Union forces and was crucial to maintaining the upper hand. Devil's Den was so named because it was one of the deadliest locations to be in, given that snipers could easily pick the enemy off from atop Little Round Top.<\/p><\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/07\/20180524_181915-1.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Dedicated in 1910, the Pennsylvania Memorial was designed by W. Liance Cottrell. It features bronze tablets on the base to honor the 34,500 Pennsylvanian men who fought in the Civil War as well as four marble tablets depicting efforts by the infantry, cavalry, artillery and the signal corps. Finally, atop the monument is the &quot;Goddess of Peace and Victory.&quot;\" data-attachment-id=\"4025\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Dedicated in 1910, the Pennsylvania Memorial was designed by W. Liance Cottrell. It features bronze tablets on the base to honor the 34,500 Pennsylvanian men who fought in the Civil War as well as four marble tablets depicting efforts by the infantry, cavalry, artillery and the signal corps. Finally, atop the monument is the &quot;Goddess of Peace and Victory.&quot;\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/07\/20180524_181915-1\/803106005.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Dedicated in 1910, the Pennsylvania Memorial was designed by W. Liance Cottrell. It features bronze tablets on the base to honor the 34,500 Pennsylvanian men who fought in the Civil War as well as four marble tablets depicting efforts by the infantry, cavalry, artillery and the signal corps. Finally, atop the monument is the \"Goddess of Peace and Victory.\"<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><\/div>\r\n\n<\/div><\/div><\/div>\n","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"","protected":false},"author":1,"featured_media":0,"comment_status":"open","ping_status":"open","sticky":false,"template":"","format":"standard","meta":{"footnotes":""},"categories":[5],"tags":[671,669,670,667,668,641],"class_list":["post-3365","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","hentry","category-travel-log","tag-civil-war","tag-declaration-of-independence","tag-gettysburg","tag-pennsylvania","tag-revolution","tag-united-states","entry"],"yoast_head":"<!-- This site is optimized with the Yoast SEO plugin v22.7 - https:\/\/yoast.com\/wordpress\/plugins\/seo\/ -->\n<title>Pennsylvania, USA - Iva Boishin<\/title>\n<meta name=\"robots\" content=\"index, follow, max-snippet:-1, max-image-preview:large, max-video-preview:-1\" \/>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/travel-log\/pennsylvania-usa\/\" \/>\n<meta property=\"og:locale\" content=\"en_US\" \/>\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\" \/>\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Pennsylvania, USA - Iva Boishin\" \/>\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https:\/\/www.ibafflatusdesign.space\/travel-log\/pennsylvania-usa\/\" \/>\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" conten